From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Wed, 6 Apr 1994 08:37:48 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-4-51] PCR/OBJ

On Tue, 29 Mar 1994, Tim Starr wrote:

> >*problematic* about the diaphanous model of consciousness, whatever that
> >might be? 
> 
> Sure: it has it that if the nature of consciousness affects the awareness of
> the objects of knowledge in any way, then it can't be authentic knowledge.

If that is correct statement of the diaphanous model, then I do not hold
the diaphanous model, and I'm also quite confident that the diaphanous
model is not *presupposed* in any of Hume's arguments, so I've completely
lost the thread of your argument. 

> >What exactly do you find problematic
> >about Hume's theory of causality, and how do those problems apply to this
> >discussion? 
> 
> The problem with Hume's theory of causality is precisely the one he made
> such a big deal out of: if it is accepted, then induction is impossible.

Well, since in fact induction (as it was commonly understood before the 
twentieth century) is impossible, I'd consider this an argument in 
*favor* of Hume's theory of causality.

Induction can be used to describe a process of generating hypotheses after
familiarizing oneself with data. But induction has nothing to do with how
we discover whether or not those hypotheses are true.  The idea that
induction *does* have something to do with the truth of the hypotheses it
generates is what Hume was showing to be impossible. 

> or belaboring the obvious.  So, to all those who would come up with any
> one of those: do your own homework, or pay me to do it for you.  I've got
> lots of other things to do besides go to lots of trouble for nothing.

But if that is your attitude, why are you posting here at all?

> >If I took the statement on faith, I would not be interested in listening
> >to your attempts at refutation, because they could make no difference to
> >my faith-based belief.
> 
> This doesn't follow.  I've experienced lots of people who are interested in
> paying attention to my refutations of their faiths, in spite of the fact
> that they can make no difference to their beliefs.

Yes, but we weren't talking about *those* people. We were talking about 
*me*.   ;)

> >To say that I have no reason to believe it is also incorrect. My claim is
> >that I cannot logically deduce it from a principle or set of principles
> >that would not themselves be subject to your demand for justification and
> >proof. That is not equivalent to a claim that I have no reason to believe
> >it. 
> 
> There's some other reason to believe it that you're hiding?

Yes, and I haven't been hiding it. I believe it because it's more 
reasonable than any of its alternatives. Now if you wish to show that you 
know of an alternative which is in fact *more* reasonable, or if you wish 
to point out exactly how my belief is unreasonable, and less reasonable 
than the alternative you have in mind, we might be getting somewhere. But 
this is exactly what you have said you are not interested in doing.

> >You have declined my invitation to refute my view,
> 
> That's partly because I'm not as interested in refuting it as understanding
> it.

Attempting a refutation is one of the best exercises you could possibly 
come up with to help you understand it. You can't refute what you don't 
understand. That's the primary source of my interest in attempting a 
refutation of "Objectivism", which I'll follow up on by reading Kelley.

> >1) The theory requires the existence of a material world
> >2) The theory is correct
> >3) therefore, a material world exists.
> >
> >That's a simple syllogism, Tim. I don't see anything circular in it at all.
> >
> >Or are you going to try to claim that we cannot find the theory correct
> >without presuming a material world?
> 
> Precisely.

Well, you went on to say a whole bunch of interesting stuff about how 
Berkeley was wrong because the contradiction of "esse est percipi" does 
not presume "esse est percipi", but you didn't say anything at all in 
support of your claim that the predictions of Darwin's theory cannot be 
compared to the empirical evidence without presuming a material world.

Despite any other reasons we might have for finding Berkeley's theory 
incorrect, if we were to suppose it true, we could still evaluate 
Darwin's theory as a scientific theory, and find it true by the standards 
of scientific truth. Having done so, we would then have an argument for 
the existence of either a) a material world or b) Berkeley's god.

This whole subthread is awfully obscure, though; here are you and I 
agreeing on the existence of a material world but differing in our 
reasons for believing in the existence of a material world. I believe in 
a material world because Darwin figured out how perceiving minds evolved 
from material objects; you believe in a material world because the 
contradiction of "esse est percipi" does not presuppose "esse est 
percipi". Whatever.

