From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 06 Apr 94 15:38:21 EDT
Subject: [#94-4-52] PCR: Gibberish Redux

Eric Forste wrote 4/6/94: <Criticism is not carried out solely by others. As far
as my own knowledge is concerned, it is carried out primarily by me.>

You have learned to criticize yourself from others.  Your use of the word
"knowledge" here is interesting considering that anything you aren't certain of
is only a belief (maybe a strongly held belief but still not knowledge).
Perhaps you are certain of more things than you previously were willing to own
up to.

Eric: <I do not rely on authority to criticize my ideas; I rely on myself to
criticize the ideas of authority, and to seek out possible refutations of my own
beliefs.>

Relying on this process of seeking out refutations of your own beliefs is how
you come to know right from wrong, from others.

Eric: <Or are you contending that everyone has the same subjective purposes? If
not, then you must be contending that there are multiple truths, different for
different people, which is just plain silly.>

As Max More wrote, people interested in PCR do have the same subjective purpose,
to approach the truth of objective reality.

Eric: <The nature of the universe, and the accuracy of my knowledge of it, is
not dependent on my values. I seek knowledge *because* I value knowledge, but my
values do not determine the *content* of my knowledge.>

It is true the nature of the universe is not dependent on your values.  It is
false that the accuracy of your knowledge of it is not dependent on your values.
Your entire consciousness is grounded in your genetic default values cohering to
survival; your rationality and the content of your knowledge is grounded in your
chosen purpose to approach the truth of the nature of the universe.  Your values
are inextricably involved in filtering and judging every thought and every
perception in your consciousness.

Eric: <When people stop seeking such criticism, they are no longer in accordance
with the prescriptions of PCR.>

Such a taskmaster!  I assume there is a time-energy budget for rationality that
prevents non-stop seeking of criticism, perhaps a cost-benefit ratio, utility.
This leads to variance of rationality within the population since each budget
and each ratio is different for each unique individual in their unique
contingent circumstances.  Judging cut-off points for pronouncing legitimate
rationality in others is difficult.

Eric: <Did you really believe that this paragraph was going to communicate
anything to anyone who hadn't read Edelman?> and <This is complete nonsense, and
I have already cited the second-level references (in Churchland's MATTER AND
CONSCIOUSNESS) to the controlled empirical experiments that demonstrate that
"subjective reality" is *not* completely accessible, not even reliably
accessible.>

See my post in reply to Max, 'PCR: The Voice of Reason' 3/30/94 for an extensive
discussion of these issues.  Also a list of references was posted, 'REF:
Epistemological Boundaries' 3/30/94 that you may be interested in.

Eric: <We can deceive ourselves about the emotions we may be feeling at any
given time (denial of jealousy, for instance).>

Then our mental state is not jealousy but denial of jealousy.  It is not in
error.

Eric: <We can hold two conflicting values, and not be sure which one is more
important to us. We can be indecisive about all kinds of subjective matters, and
this indecision reflects very real uncertainty.>

Then our mental state is uncertain.  It is not in error.

Eric: <The empirical experiments I second-level cited above even show that we
can deceive ourselves about sensory qualia.>

See reply to Max referenced above.  We can have difficulty expressing our mental
state to others, but our mental states are true.  We cannot observe our own
mental states, there is no distinction between subject and object, no difference
between appearance and reality.  We can't even observe our attempts at
observing.

Eric: <The ultimate criticism is when the universe kills you for doing something
stupid.>

This sounds like Heraclitus:  In the end, "strife is justice"; the competition
of individuals, groups, species, institutions, and empires constitutes nature's
supreme court, from whose verdict there is no appeal.

Eric: <That's called natural selection.  That's what epistemology is "grounded"
in, because that's the process that created our minds and all their
epistemological apparatus in the first place.>

Epistemology is grounded in consciousness, purpose lies at the base of
consciousness.  Our free choice of purpose supervenes on top of this genetic
default purpose, it is linked but it's a higher spontaneous order than
biological evolution.  Rationality is grounded in the consciously chosen purpose
of approaching the truth of nature.

Eric: <I mean, sure, *now* memetic evolution proceeds within "consensual
reality", but where did the first memes come from?>

Gerald Edelman, in his book "Bright Air, Brilliant Fire: On the Matter of the
Mind" (1993) draws on the work of:
- Lakoff, G. "Women, Fire, and Dangerous Things: What Categories Reveal About
the Mind" (1987) and
- Johnson, M. "The Body in the Mind: The Bodily Basis of Meaning, Imagination,
and Reason" (1987) and
- Langacker, R.W. "Foundations of Cognitive Grammar" (1987)

He discusses cognitive grammar and shows that embodiment of our minds is the
basis of meaning, and shows how our minds are embodied.  He includes a nice
evolutionary account of when meaning arose as modules of primary consciousness
and higher consciousness were selected.

Eric: <Okay, so you're arguing tabula rasa.>

No, I am arguing the importance of epigenetic influences during our development
of consciousness, especially in learning about our bodies and in the social
interactions that bring about linguistic abilities.  From conception on, not
just after birth.  Induction in early embryonic development is the same as
induction in later child development, formation of neuronal pathways and
reentrant maps hooked to the genetic homeostatic value systems.  Again, I
recommend a reading of Edelman's Theory of Neuronal Group Selection which is
detailed and extensive and will not be posted here by me.

Eric: <I sound smug because discussing this stuff with you has 
convinced me that I understand epistemology much better than you do.>

I am fully prepared to accede to the justification of your smugness if I am
given some evidence supporting your judgment of our relative degrees of
understanding of epistemology.  In the meantime, I feel comfortable with Max's
ideas about PCR's contextual certainty, defense against relativism, and
grounding in subjective purpose.  Our discussion has led to this clarification
for me, even if you didn't directly provide it.  It is unfortunate that my
arguments seem like "gibberish" to you.  When this occurs, it generally means
that people haven't honed their knowledge with the same contingent processes of
criticism.  Too much to know, too little time.  Or is that, too much to
"believe"?  :)

