From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Wed, 6 Apr 94 14:32:21 EDT
Subject: [#94-4-53] Land vs Other Property

I'd like to turn Dan Davis' questions around a couple ways.  These are meant
as brain-teasers not as taunts, and directed at everyone, not just Dan.
Personally, I think libertarian property-rights theory could use some
examining, mostly around the issue of arbitrariness.

Why should I respect someone's right to non-land property any
differently from the way I do land property?  Why should people
be able to keep *anything* without compensating me?

Why *shouldn't* I respect the ability to coerce?

Whose ideas of a proper setup should prevail?  Isn't there going to be a
combination of agreement, coercion and acquiescence no matter what?
If so, what's a good way to proceed?

Why, after all, should the world be anything like what we want?

But why shouldn't it?  Why not say, forget everyone else, we're the smart,
politically hip, fast-moving ones, let's set it up the way *we* want?

If you think there should be some sort of equality between your/our 
wishes and other people's, what the heck for?  Does it have something to
do with workability or getting other people to agree to it?  If so, aren't
you bowing down before their ability to coerce you?

Suppose my respect for others' claims to property (both in land and other
stuff) has to do with their eventually, overall, probably doing something
worthwhile with it that makes my world a better place.  Isn't that just as
base a motivation as wanting to avoid their coercion?

Who should be compensated or respected for anything?  Unborn people?
Brain-dead people on life support who have willed the compensation to others?
Bankrupt people on very expensive life support?  Dolphins?  On what basis
do you choose?  Why do you have a right to an opinion on the matter?

