From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Thu, 07 Apr 94 02:21:20 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-54] PCR: Smug Nihilism

Eric Forste replies to Reilly Jones:
>> Subjective reality is completely accessible to us and us alone,
>> the domain of purpose and value.  
>
>This is complete nonsense, and I have already cited the second-level 
>references (in Churchland's MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS) to the controlled 
>empirical experiments that demonstrate that "subjective reality" is *not* 
>completely accessible, not even reliably accessible.

The difference here seems to arise from the fact that the eliminative
materialists are mainly interested in a subset of objective reality --
an impartial, objective assessment of an individual's brain states.  Or
rather, what eliminative materialists think another person's mental states
really are.  On the other hand, speaking about subjective reality, I'd
favour the dictionary interpretation which Reilly seems to be taking;

subjective a. 1. based on personal feelings, not impartial 2. of the self
3. existing in the mind

I would hold subjective reality to be what I think or feel to be mental
states, existing in my mind.   Since what I think actually is what I think,
what I think is subjective reality actually is my subjective reality.  But
due to the epistemological limits, it is possible that these thoughts and
feelings are an inaccurate perception of brain states, particularly with
very short term sensations and qualia that are too similar to discern.
Though there are certain long term beliefs that we can be certain of.  If
some 'expert', for instance, claimed to know that I'm really in favour of
deathism, I'd classify him as a crank.

>And the truth of everything I've said in this paragraph shows that
>"subjective reality" is 
>a myth, that the states and perceptions we refer to as "subjective 
>reality" are just a conveniently-labeled subset of objective reality.

It's a myth in the sense that it's only subjective; but the qualifying
"subjective" should distinguish it from objective reality.  So we have
subjective reality which is inner thoughts and feelings, and objective
brain states which might not always tie up if eliminative materialism is
correct.  I don't think the eliminative materialists are denying the
existence of subjective reality (which is very real to the subject).  If
so, they may just as well be known as "crypto-behaviourists". 

