From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Wed, 6 Apr 1994 21:18:50 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-55] Grigsby's Fallacies

>From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
>
>This messge is basically another dissection of Tim's specious methods
>of argument, with little informational content.

"Specious" apparently defined as "disliked by Grigsby."

>  Feel free to skip unless
>you want to familiarize yourself with another technique of diversion,
>namely "So?", and refamiliarize yourself with the old favorites;
>diversion via hairsplitting, random inconsistency, and strawman.

The "hairsplitting" charge is nothing more than the counter-attack used
against those who point out package-deals, such as the combination of a
reasonable position (such as the claim that CFCs may increase the size
and duration of the Antarctic Ozone hole) with an unreasonable one (such
as the inference that CFCs are thus causing a significant amount of harm).
Package-dealing has a far worse history than anything Grigsby's charged me
with: it was pioneered by the Marxists, who used it in combination with
their attack on the efficacy of reason as nothing more than bourgeois
superstition to deflect rational scrutiny of their positions.  This tactic
has penetrated the culture enough now that people of many diverse ideological
persuasions use it, including some self-styled anarchists.

The "random inconsistency" charge is simply unsubstantiated.  The "strawman"
charge is unjustified because there's no evidence that I intended to mis-
represent Grigsby's position - possibly because that wasn't my intent.  The
civil thing to do when one's position is misrepresented is not to relexively
accuse the one who did it of committing the strawman fallacy, but to point
out the mistake and correct it.

And now on to the substance of Grigsby's flame:

>...I probably should have been more clear...
>...damn if this strawman isn't the centerpiece of your argument.  

Here we have excerpts which indicate admission of guilt for unclarity, along
with an inconsistent accusation that I got his position wrong on purpose out
of malevolence.

>...1) Some quality of the air in the LA basin causes lung damage.
>2) Cars (internal-combustion) are a major contributor to pollution 
>   in the LA basin.
>3) The effects on people of certain contaminants in car exhaust are
>   consistent with the observed effects in 1).

Here we have what Grigsy claims to be both a sound argument and the one he
already made, which is neither.  His previous argument was not that both LA
air and car exhaust caused lung damage, although that may well have been
his intent.  His previous argument was that both cause harm, therefore car
exhaust causes LA air to be harmful, as far as I could make it out.  The
specific comparison he made was between breating LA air and breathing car
exhaust.  I'm willing to accept that breathing LA air causes lung damage, but
if Grigsby think that's what breathing car exhaust does, then I invite him to
try the following experiment: park a car in a garage, close the door, connect
a hose to the exhaust pipe, put the other end in through the crack of one
window, roll the rest up, tape up what space is left for gas to escape in
the window that isn't rolled up all the way, get into the driver's seat,
start the car, and sit there for half an hour.  If all that happens to him is
that he gets "lung damage," then I'll concede the point.

If there was some evidence proffered that already supported the claim that
the effects of LA air and car exhaust are similar, I missed it - possibly
because none was forthcoming.

>The measurements you kindly provided some time ago which show pollution
>declining starting in the late 70's (just after the main air-quality
>legislation was passed) support this logic.

This results from a consistent misreading of my past claim, which was not that
LA air TSPs started decreasing in the late 70s, but in the 1930s.  Grigsby got
it wrong the first time, and evidently missed my correction of his mistake
the first time.  Since this began four decades prior to the legislation he
worships, it can hardly have been caused by it - unless there's been some new
miraculous spin-off on ex post facto laws :-).  The explanation which occurs to
me is that the cause of this was real estate development, but I have no proof
of it.

>...The location of car exhaust does not cause it to become intrinsically
>intrinsically less harmful.

I wasn't speaking of its location, but its concentration, or doseage.
Ever since Paracelsus, medical science has known that it's the doseage
that makes the poison.  Almost everything is poisonous in high enough
doseage, and almost everything is not poisonous in low enough doseage.
This indicates that even if you can show that high doses of car exhaust,
such as that in the experiment I outlined above, is harmful, it still
doesn't mean that low doses of it, such as those found in car exhaust
distributed over the entire LA basin (care to guess how many parts per
million that is?), are also harmful.  It may well be perfectly safe; the
burden of proof rests with those who want to prove it poisonous.

>I refuse to engage in further dialogue with you if you are going to demand
>demand absolute certainty, which it appears that you are.

A mere preponderance of the evidence will suffice.  You simply haven't come
close.

>Would it help if I had a "Dr." in front of my name, or something?

Nope.  It will only help if you had any support for your claims.

>Your analogy is faulty; you are assuming that only one cause can exist
>for the harm (ex. killing Kennedy).

Not at all.  To make the analogy to a multi-causal theory, then all we
have to do is change your role from that of the agent who pulled the trigger
to one who was part of a conspiracy to assasinate Kennedy.  Then your pre-
sumption that car exhaust is what makes LA air cause lung damage becomes
analogous to presuming that you were part of an assasination conspiracy.

I think this is the central issue: should we presume man-made substances
harmful or not?  I say not.  At the risk of being accused of another strawman
fallacy, Grigsby seems to say yes.  All I want to know is: why?

>Your argument is not that we're blaming the wrong thing; your argument
>(to use the analogy) is that Kennedy isn't dead, and anyway Oswald didn't
>kill him.

This is a misrepresentation of my position.  I don't deny the effects, I
question the causal attribution.  Perhaps we should presume you guilty of
making strawmen, as long as we're going to presume that car exhaust makes
LA air cause lung damage.

>> Who's talking about legislative action?  I've been talking about judicial
>> action.
>
>Both enforced by the state.

Not necessarily.  Pfeh.  Some anarchist!  Can't even imagine the possibility
of a non-State judiciary.

